Choose a system and track heat, work, and enthalpy (@gc-energy: 0, is #chapter, #part-of @general-chemistry, #source "../sources/Chemistry-OpenStax.lingua#r_02Y5N2VMTTPBDSNYPD4Z26EZ94", #source "../sources/Chem1-Lower.lingua#r_0NFQA43WZVMECZY6SKZJMRXWBZ", #source "../sources/IUPAC-Green-Book.lingua#r_5SR8CP04E580VSV69MYQNSZ73F", #general-chemistry "Calorimetry-and-thermal-models.lingua#r_55B9CZ9RST5E8CANA6GZ0SNPEY", #thermodynamics-and-heat-transfer "../../../physics/thermodynamics-and-heat-transfer/foundations/Heat-and-work-signs-across-physics-and-chemistry.lingua#r_3Z4G73ZME2A7M1A63NGX177SQN") { r_545JZM2734935DFWXBV90WM5PE
**Read:** [Chemistry 2e — Flowers, Theopold, Langley, and Robinson](../sources/Chemistry-OpenStax.lingua#r_02Y5N2VMTTPBDSNYPD4Z26EZ94), chapter 5 in full; [Chem1 General Chemistry Virtual Textbook — Stephen Lower](../sources/Chem1-Lower.lingua#r_0NFQA43WZVMECZY6SKZJMRXWBZ), Chemical Energetics completely; [Quantities, Units and Symbols in Physical Chemistry — IUPAC Green Book](../sources/IUPAC-Green-Book.lingua#r_5SR8CP04E580VSV69MYQNSZ73F), energy, work, heat, and thermodynamic quantities.

Define the system, surroundings, boundary, initial/final states, and allowed exchanges before applying an energy equation. Under the chemistry sign convention, ΔU = q + w, with energy transferred into the system counted positively. Pressure-volume work for expansion against an external pressure is w = −∫p_ext dV. Other kinds of work need their own terms.

Internal energy is a state quantity; heat and work describe transfers associated with a process. Temperature is another quantity with a different role. A large body and a small body at the same temperature need not contain the same energy or undergo the same temperature change after receiving the same heat.

Enthalpy H = U + pV is useful for chemical changes under specified pressure conditions. For a closed system at constant pressure with only pressure-volume work, heat transfer is ΔH. State these conditions instead of applying q = ΔH to any vessel. A reaction enthalpy also specifies reaction equation, species, phases, temperature, and reference conditions.

**Work:** assign signs for heating, expansion, electrical work, and cooling under one consistent convention. Derive the constant-pressure relation from the first law and H definition. Compare a constant-volume calorimetric interpretation with a constant-pressure one and identify the additional information needed to relate them.

Read the complete thermochemistry chapter and the Chem1 energetics unit, including exercises. Later entropy and free energy explain direction under constraints; a favorable enthalpy alone is insufficient to determine equilibrium or reaction speed. Keep energy transfer, available work, and chemical conversion distinguishable throughout the later industrial studies.

